WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:28.400
The treaty that healed the ozone layer accidentally put a climate accelerator in the world's air conditioners. This is unintended consequences, episode 98. In the spring of 1994, automobile plants from Michigan to mines began charging new car air conditioners with a refrigerant advertised as ozone safe. The chlorine was gone. The chemistry that had been chewing a hole in the sky over Antarctica could not run that reaction anymore.

00:00:28.400 --> 00:00:51.340
This was designed to close the most famous wound human industry had ever opened in the atmosphere. Instead, the replacement gases, hydroflotocarbons, harmless to ozone, turned out to be greenhouse gases hundreds to thousands of times more potent than carbon dioxide. And they were being installed in a technology the warming planet was about to demand in the billions.

00:00:51.920 --> 00:01:09.120
To see why that swap looked like a triumph, you have to go back to the emergency the diplomats were actually hired to stop. The people who built the Montreal Protocol were not fumbling in the dark and they were not chasing a fashionable cause. They were answering a specific measured emergency.

00:01:09.120 --> 00:01:29.720
In June of 1974, Mario Molina and F. Sherwood Roland published a paper in Nature. The paper showed that chlorofluoro carbons, CFCs, the miracle compounds Thomas Midgley Jr. had demonstrated in 1930 by inhaling a lung full and blowing out a candle could drift intact into the stratosphere.

00:01:29.720 --> 00:01:43.120
Up there, a CFC molecule would shed chlorine and catalytically destroy ozone. A single chlorine atom could scavenge tens of thousands of ozone molecules. For a decade, the finding was contested.

00:01:43.480 --> 00:01:55.220
Then, in May of 1985, Joe Farman, Brian Gardner, and Jonathan Shanklin of the British Antarctic Survey reported in nature that October ozone over Halle Bay had collapsed.

00:01:55.220 --> 00:02:09.960
Their aging dobs and spectrophotometer was measuring what NASA's satellites had been discarding as instrument error. The hole was real. Skin cancer projections, crop damage estimates, and the fate of phytoplankton were no longer thought experiments.

00:02:10.880 --> 00:02:19.820
Diplomats already had a framework from the 1985 Vienna Convention. What they needed was a control treaty and they needed it before the chemistry ran away from them.

00:02:20.820 --> 00:02:34.400
In that light, the brief was rational and narrow. Stop putting chlorine and bromine into the stratosphere as fast as sovereign states would accept, without grounding the world's refrigeration, foam blowing, and electronics cleaning overnight.

00:02:35.020 --> 00:02:47.680
Climate change was on the intellectual map. James Hansen would testify before the United States Senate in June of 1988, three years after Vienna, and a year after the diplomats sat down in Montreal.

00:02:47.680 --> 00:02:59.060
Climate was not the mandate on the table in those rooms, and the atmospheric lifetime and heat-trapping power of the substitutes had not been the axis on which a replacement would be judged.

00:02:59.700 --> 00:03:07.220
That narrow brief became a treaty, a fund, and an industry research program optimized for exactly one atmospheric number.

00:03:07.220 --> 00:03:18.080
On September 16, 1987, 24 countries and the European economic community signed the Montreal Protocol on substances that deplete the ozone layer.

00:03:18.800 --> 00:03:30.500
The first text was cautious, more freeze than funeral. Production of key CFCs would be cut 50% by 1998. That restraint was not timidity so much as coalition building.

00:03:30.500 --> 00:03:45.600
Mostafa Tolba at the United Nations Environment Program, UNEP, and negotiators including Richard Benedict of the United States, needed chemical companies, developing country governments, and skeptical ministries of industry in the same room.

00:03:46.320 --> 00:03:54.540
The science then did the accelerating. The 1990 London amendment moved developed countries toward a full CFC phase out by 2000.

00:03:55.300 --> 00:04:04.340
Copenhagen in 1992 pulled that date forward to 1996 and put the transitional hydrochloro-florocarbons on a longer leash.

00:04:05.100 --> 00:04:12.740
A multilateral fund, created in London, began paying the agreed incremental costs for article five countries to convert factories.

00:04:13.420 --> 00:04:22.960
DuPont, which had resisted controls through much of the 1980s, announced after the 1988 ozone trends panel that it would stop making CFCs.

00:04:22.960 --> 00:04:28.540
The industry's research engine turned toward substitutes that had no ozone depletion potential.

00:04:29.180 --> 00:04:51.780
Hydro-florocarbons, HFC-134A, for car air conditioners, and domestic refrigerators, later high global warming potential blends such as R-410A for building cooling, looked on the criteria that mattered in those rooms, almost ideal, non-flammable, low toxicity, thermodynamically close to the chemicals they replaced,

00:04:51.780 --> 00:04:59.400
and a chlorine atom of zero. Proponents described the switch as the proof that a global environmental treaty could work.

00:04:59.940 --> 00:05:04.400
Skeptics of the ozone science had mostly gone quiet by the mid-1990s.

00:05:04.840 --> 00:05:16.000
What almost no one treated as a design constraint was the heat those fluorine-rich molecules would trap if they leaked, or if they were manufactured at the scale of a planet buying its first air conditioner.

00:05:16.000 --> 00:05:32.240
The Kyoto Protocol would list HFCs among its six greenhouse gases in 1997, but Kyoto bound only developed country totals, and Kyoto did not stop Montreal's fund from financing the very conversions that locked HFCs in.

00:05:32.860 --> 00:05:41.040
On ozone, the design worked. On climate, the same design choices became the problem. The ozone layer began slowly to heal.

00:05:41.040 --> 00:05:49.480
That part of the story is not a prelude. It is a genuine success, and any honest account has to hold it in the same hand as what followed.

00:05:50.260 --> 00:05:59.640
Assessments by the World Meteorological Organization and UNEP now project that mid-latitude ozone will return to 1980 levels around 2040.

00:05:59.640 --> 00:06:17.980
The Arctic around 2045. The Antarctic around 2066. Modeling of a world avoided, the phrase researchers used for the counterfactual in which CFCs were never controlled suggests a 21st century of catastrophic ozone loss and dangerous ultraviolet radiation.

00:06:17.980 --> 00:06:30.060
Kofi Anan called Montreal perhaps the single most successful international agreement to date, and on Montreal's own terms he was not wrong. The trouble lived in the replacement rule.

00:06:30.760 --> 00:06:46.140
HFCs do not eat ozone. They do, however, absorb infrared radiation with startling efficiency. HFC 134A has a 100 year global warming potential in the neighborhood of 1,300 times that of carbon dioxide.

00:06:46.140 --> 00:07:02.420
The blend that became standard in residential air conditioners, R410A, sits near 2,000. HFC 23, a byproduct of other fluorochemile manufacturing, is in the range of 12,000 to 14,600.

00:07:03.420 --> 00:07:17.060
Because the molecules were screened for ozone depletion potential, toxicity and flammability, not for radiative forcing, the industry had optimized a refrigerant for one atmospheric problem and delivered a concentrated dose of another.

00:07:17.660 --> 00:07:28.960
The causal chain tightened because the world was not holding still. As CFC plants closed, HFC production scaled to fill every niche the old chemicals had occupied and then some.

00:07:28.960 --> 00:07:38.840
The multilateral fund, doing exactly the job it was written to do, helped convert production lines in China, India and elsewhere toward ozone safe gases.

00:07:39.600 --> 00:07:51.560
Those conversions were victories at the factory gate. They were also path dependence. Once a nation's appliance industry, service technicians and safety codes are built around a non-flammable HFC blend.

00:07:51.560 --> 00:08:01.860
The cheaper, climate-friendly or alternatives, hydrocarbons such as propane, ammonia, carbon dioxide look like a fire code problem and a retraining bill.

00:08:02.500 --> 00:08:14.800
Meanwhile, the demand curve bent upward. The international energy agency estimated in 2018 that the global stock of air conditioners in buildings stood at about 1.6 billion units.

00:08:14.800 --> 00:08:29.000
That stock could reach 5.6 billion by 2050. Most of the growth would be in countries that were hot, urbanizing and, thanks in part to Montreal's own success, newly equipped with HFC supply chains.

00:08:29.720 --> 00:08:37.600
Every leaked kilogram during manufacture, installation, use and disposal was a pulse of high global warming potential gas.

00:08:38.600 --> 00:08:46.960
Every intact kilogram still implied a compressor drawing electricity, often from coal, in the places where cooling demand was rising fastest.

00:08:47.700 --> 00:08:55.960
That is the second order effect hiding inside the first. The ozone fix did not merely swap one molecule for a worse climate molecule.

00:08:56.460 --> 00:09:04.560
It standardized a cooling architecture just as climate change itself was making cooling feel less like a comfort good and more like infrastructure.

00:09:05.560 --> 00:09:19.680
By the late 2000s, the numbers were no longer a footnote. Work led by goose welders and colleagues projected that, without new controls, HFC radiative forcing could become a substantial fraction of the climate problem by mid-century.

00:09:20.260 --> 00:09:28.760
In high growth scenarios of that period, the burden sat on the order of several hundred billion tons of carbon dioxide equivalent over coming decades.

00:09:29.760 --> 00:09:44.080
Papers then suggested HFCs might contribute on the order of 0.25 to 0.40 watts per square meter of forcing by 2050, or emissions comparable to a significant percentage of global carbon dioxide.

00:09:44.080 --> 00:09:58.560
The irony had a human texture, a refrigerator factory that had been paid to stop using CFCs could be, in the same decade, a rising source of climate pollution while its government received credit for protecting the ozone layer.

00:09:59.220 --> 00:10:06.620
A family in Guangzhou or Hyderabad buying a first split air conditioner was making a rational decision about heat and health.

00:10:06.620 --> 00:10:13.780
The molecule inside the copper was the blind spot of a treaty that had been asked to solve one crisis at a time.

00:10:14.620 --> 00:10:21.780
Institutional siloing made the mist durable. Montréal's institutions measured ozone depletion potential tons.

00:10:22.440 --> 00:10:32.100
Kyoto's measured basket greenhouse gases in countries that were not by then, the center of HFC growth. No single secretariat owned the interaction.

00:10:32.100 --> 00:10:39.000
The third order effect was political as much as chemical. Success itself became a reason to stop looking.

00:10:39.740 --> 00:10:49.400
When a treaty is called the greatest environmental agreement ever written, the substitutes it blessed inherit some of that halo, until the atmospheric record says otherwise.

00:10:49.400 --> 00:11:10.780
The correction when it came used the original machine. Scientists, the EPA's old ozone hands, advocates such as Derwood-Zelke and a coalition of island states and African governments spent the early 2010s arguing that Montréal, not a new climate treaty, was the competent body to phase down the replacement.

00:11:10.780 --> 00:11:18.980
The legal hook was real, HFC's are not ozone depletors, but they exist as a direct consequence of ozone policy.

00:11:19.580 --> 00:11:38.480
And Montréal already had universal ratification, a multilateral fund, and a habit of tightening itself. On October 15, 2016, in Kigali, Rwanda, the parties adopted an amendment to phase down HFC production and consumption by more than 80% over roughly 30 years.

00:11:38.480 --> 00:11:48.380
The schedules were staggered, developed countries first, China and most developing countries next, a later group including India and the Gulf states after that.

00:11:48.960 --> 00:11:59.760
Estimates from UNEP and partner assessments suggest that full implementation could avoid about 0.3 to 0.5 degrees Celsius of warming by 2100.

00:11:59.760 --> 00:12:12.900
The amendment entered into force on January 1, 2019. The United States wrote a domestic HFC phase down into the American Innovation and Manufacturing Act in December of 2020.

00:12:13.620 --> 00:12:24.060
The Senate ratified Kigali in September of 2022. The ozone holes architects had, unusually, been allowed to debug their own unintended consequence.

00:12:24.060 --> 00:12:34.980
The meta-irony is already visible at the edges. The next generation substitutes, hydro-floro-olephans, have very low global warming potentials, which is the point.

00:12:35.640 --> 00:12:45.080
But some break down into tri-floro-acetic acid, and researchers are still arguing over how much of that acid's accumulation in water and soil should worry us.

00:12:45.800 --> 00:12:52.180
Hydro-carbons and carbon dioxide avoid that fluorine tail, and then collide with fire codes and training.

00:12:53.180 --> 00:13:06.420
Illegal trade and phased out gases has cropped up more than once. That includes a well-documented burst of unexpected CFC 11 emissions, later traced largely to eastern China and then after enforcement reduced.

00:13:07.220 --> 00:13:18.800
None of that undoes Kigali. It does mean the thermostat problem was not a single bad swap. It is a recurring test of whether a fix is evaluated on every axis the Earth actually cares about.

00:13:18.800 --> 00:13:28.580
Three principles sit in the chemistry. First, a solution scored on one environmental axis will migrate risk onto the axes you did not measure.

00:13:29.320 --> 00:13:38.540
Ozone depletion potential was the right emergency metric in 1987, and it was still an incomplete spec for a molecule that would be manufactured by the Megaton.

00:13:39.540 --> 00:13:51.220
Second, the substitute inherits the original system's growth curve. Once you bless a chemical that fits existing factories, safety codes, and business models, you have not merely replaced a gas.

00:13:51.920 --> 00:13:54.940
You have underwritten the expansion of the machine that uses it.

00:13:55.620 --> 00:14:05.660
Third, institutions can correct themselves, but only if someone is responsible for the interaction between treaties, funds, and markets rather than for a single pollutant.

00:14:05.660 --> 00:14:16.140
The useful question is not whether the diplomats in Montreal should have seen 2016 from 1987. They were preventing a different catastrophe with the evidence they had.

00:14:16.840 --> 00:14:24.180
The live question sits in this decade. Look at the replacements we are celebrating right now. New cooling molecules, again.

00:14:25.180 --> 00:14:36.560
Battery chemistry is sold as the clean successor. Carbon removal system scored on tons captured. Forever chemical swaps designed to get one band substance out of a product and onto a shelf.

00:14:37.380 --> 00:14:42.500
What I would watch for is not whether the replacement is better on the number that got the press release written.

00:14:42.840 --> 00:14:51.280
I would watch whether anyone owns the other axes. The growth curve of the machine that will use the substitute, the fund or subsidy that locks it in,

00:14:51.280 --> 00:14:57.600
the fire code and the training bill, the breakdown products nobody measured because they were not the emergency.

00:14:58.340 --> 00:15:05.080
And I would ask who, by name or by office, is assigned to look at that interaction before the next megaton ships.

00:15:05.880 --> 00:15:10.200
That's the case for today. The lessons tend to travel further when you share them.

00:15:10.600 --> 00:15:16.100
So send this one to someone who'd find it useful. And subscribe wherever you're listening. See you tomorrow.

